Decision page for the architecture review on 2026-10-21.

kpi Peak load | 1,800 msg/s
kpi Average load | 400 msg/s
kpi Message size | < 8 KB
kpi Delivery | at-least-once | acceptable

Ordering is needed only per order, not globally.

# Options
- **SQS**: fully managed AWS queue, with FIFO queues for ordered delivery per message group.
- **Kafka (Amazon MSK)**: managed Kafka brokers with partitioned, replayable logs.
- **RabbitMQ (Amazon MQ)**: managed RabbitMQ broker with flexible exchange-based routing.

# Comparison
table = Option,Ordering,Throughput ceiling,Ops burden,Replay,Monthly cost; SQS,FIFO per message group,very high,none,no,$310; Kafka,per partition,very high,high,yes up to retention,"$1,450"; RabbitMQ,per queue,medium,medium,no,$690

data cost = option,usd; SQS,310; Kafka,1450; RabbitMQ,690
chart bar cost unit=$ labels title="Estimated monthly cost"

# Pros and cons
## SQS
- Pro: fully managed, nothing to operate.
- Pro: scales automatically.
- Con: no replay.
- Con: 256 KB message limit.

## Kafka (Amazon MSK)
- Pro: replay up to retention.
- Pro: supports stream processing.
- Con: needs partition planning.
- Con: we own broker upgrades.

## RabbitMQ (Amazon MQ)
- Pro: flexible routing.
- Con: we would own capacity planning.
- Con: lower throughput ceiling than the other two.

# Recommendation
! Use **Kafka (Amazon MSK) with topics partitioned by order ID**. It gives the per-order ordering we need, covers our peak load, and supports replay up to retention, at about $1,450 a month.

!! Kafka needs a dedicated on-call rotation for the brokers.
